  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 PM MDT Sat May 16 2026\n\nAFTERNOON STORMS AND GUSTY WINDS:\n\nA trough is digging into the Great Basin this afternoon, with flow \naloft turning southwesterly. This is beginning to advect some deeper \nmoisture into the Four Corners region, along with breezy \nsouthwesterly winds gusting 30-40 mph. Modest instability in the \nform of 200-500 J/Kg of CAPE and steepening lapse rates, along with \nthe increased upper level moisture, will lead to isolated to widely \nscattered showers and thunderstorms this afternoon. Surface levels \nremain quite dry, however, leading to inverted-V profiles that favor \nstrong, gusty outflow winds. These gusty winds, along with the \npotential for lightning, could exacerbate critical fire weather \nconditions that already exist along the I-70 corridor and across the \ndesert valleys of central-eastern Utah and central-western Colorado. \nRed Flag Warnings are in effect for these areas through 8PM this \nevening.\n\n\nCOOLER AND WETTER:\n\nShower activity is expected to continue into the overnight hours \ntonight as a cold front drops into northeast Utah and northwest \nColorado. With surface levels finally saturating, the main threat \nwill transition away from winds and towards wetting rainfall, with \nsnow at the higher elevations. Overnight lows remain mild, but as \nthe cold front sags south tomorrow, highs will take a hit. North of \nI-70, behind the front and under clouds and showers, highs will run \naround 10 degrees below normal. To the south, highs will run closer \nto normal for the middle of May, with precipitation increasing in \ncoverage as the day progresses. \n\nThe core of the low is expected to track over the Four Corners \ntomorrow night into Monday, bringing the highest precipitation rates \nof the storm cycle. Much colder air will see snow levels dropping to \naround 8500 feet by Monday morning, although any substantial snow \naccumulations will remain above 9500 feet. Most ranges will see 2-4 \ninches of fresh snow by Tuesday morning. The Eastern Uintas and Park \nRange are looking most favored with this system, with amounts of 4-8 \ninches expected. This could impact travel over the mountain passes, \nas well as along any recently opened seasonal roads in these areas. \nUse precaution if travelling Sunday evening through Monday \nafternoon. Temperatures take a nosedive on Monday as well, with \nhighs topping out 10-20 degrees below normal.\n\nFREEZE POTENTIAL:\n\nPrecipitation will hang up on the Divide Tuesday and Wednesday with \nsome afternoon showers left over in the northwest flow behind the \ndeparting trough. The rest of the area should begin to dry out \nTuesday and Wednesday but below normal temperatures remain. Freezing \ntemperatures will likely impact portions of the CWA Tuesday and \nWednesday morning behind this cold front.",
